This was prompted by the "best class" thread, where a number of
questions were raised regarding how to interpret data gained from
nethack.alt.org regarding games played, ascension percentages, etc.

nethack.alt.org is probably our best universal, centralized source for
any sort of data analysis regarding Nethack games; its nature prevents
save-scumming and other outside-of-game cheating, and it is a consistent
platform across multiple players, something sufficiently rare in the
community.  However, data garnered from there has a few obvious flaws.

First, the data is a self-selecting pool.  People choose based on their
own personal reasons which class to play and how often; there are
players with double-digit Wizard ascensions who have never ascended
certain roles.  This tends to skew things just a touch and must be
compensated for.

Second, things such as quitscumming and low-score suiciding are rampant
for certain roles, further skewing the data.  So if we're going to try
to do anything, we'll need to be sure to normalize the data as best as
possible.  One obvious method is by stripping out all the quits and
escapes, as those clearly indicate a non-attempt at winning.

Third, how does one define skill, anyway, when score is useless?

The data used in this analysis was taken from nethack.alt.org's logfile
on the morning of Sunday, February 25, 2007.  It contains, to the best
of my knowledge, all games played on nethack.alt.org up to that date.
The tables are all filtered to include only games from version 3.4.3 for
consistency's sake; this is why the stats in the bottom table do not
precisely match the full stat report from NAO.

The data can be found at this link:
http://www.akrasiac.org/rachel/role_distinctions.html

An overall explanation:

- -- One of the more obvious means to determine "skill" is by whether or
not the player has ascended, and how many times.  The first 7 columns
are exactly that; how many games were ascended under that role by a
player who has ascended at least "X" times.  So, the third column in the
first row would be "how many ascensions for Arc were by a player who has
ascended at least 3 times".

- -- It is well-known that there is a certain subset of players who can be
defined as "serial ascenders"; players who have ascended each role
multiple times.  These players would potentially skew the dataset, and
so it would be wise to find a way to filter them out as best as
possible.  For this analysis' purposes, a "serial ascender" was defined
as a player who has 13 or more ascensions; there are 54 of those players
on NAO.  Any columns referencing "serial" are assumed to be only
referring to those players' statistics; any column referencing
"non-serial" are statistics with those players' games specifically
filtered out.

- -- The first table has been normalized by removing all duplicate
player/role combinations for all games.  In other words, no matter how
many times Bob played a Wizard, he shows up only once in the file, and
accordingly once in the table.  So for, say, Rangers, this means that
exactly 122 different people ascended a Ranger in version 3.4.3.  The
second table is pure cumulative numbers with the same column
transformations applied, meaning that Bob's 17 Wizard ascensions all
show up.

- -- The green highlights mark the highest number in each column; the red
highlights mark the lowest number.

- -- The # 1st Asc column is the number of ascensions for each role that
were the corresponding player's first ever ascension on NAO.

- -- Finally, there are two columns at the end named AAPF and AAPF/NS.
These were added upon the sudden realization that there is one major
truth which can wreck our data completely; that is, _the first ascension
is the hardest, regardless of class._

Think about that one again.  The first ascension is ALWAYS the hardest.
 Some deaths are common to all classes and are not even remotely
indicative of the class difficulty; the "floating eye death", the
"closed shopkeeper door" death... All of these will typically occur
before a player's first ascension, and then rarely afterwards if ever.
So we need to find a way to filter that out as best as possible, and the
AAPF columns are just that.  It stands for "Average Ascensions Prior to
First", and means exactly what it sounds like; for each player who has
ascended a given class, the number of ascensions for that player prior
to that player's first ascension in the class was calculated and
averaged.  (The /NS is, of course, with our serial ascenders removed.)

Rough conclusions drawn:

- -- Wizards really are the most common ascension on NAO, no matter how
you slice it.  59 more distinct players ascended a Wizard than the next
most common, Valkyrie.  29 more players have a Wizard as their "first".

- -- Some classes are clearly ascended far more often by "less
experienced" players; comparing our top and bottom ascension counts for
"at least one ascension" and "two or more ascensions", we see that 109
ascensions for Wizard were some player's ONLY ascension, while only 2
ascensions for Caveman were.  The dropoff for some classes as you
increase the filtering is extremely sharp, where for others it's
relatively flat. The "non-serial" column in both tables is also an
excellent indicator of just how much influence the serial ascenders have
on the statistics.

- -- For serial ascenders (the 13+ column), the difficulty difference
between classes is almost completely insignificant -- the variations in
data in the top table can likely be ascribed to the self-selection
process.  The variations in the bottom table show up primarily in the
conventional-wisdom "easy" classes, unsurprisingly.

- -- It's interesting to see how different the ascension percentage per
role looks in the unique table compared to the raw data -- and also how
different the rankings look for non-serial ascenders compared to total
percentages.  For comparison, the role rankings for top/bottom:

Unique-games, unique-ascensions by non-serials:
Val, Wiz, Tou, Bar, Pri, Sam, Arc, Rog, Hea, Mon, Ran, Cav, Kni

full-data ascensions by non-serials:
Cav, Kni, Bar, Val, Tou, Sam, Hea, Rog, Ran, Pri, Arc, Wiz, Mon

The sharp difference between these two (especially Cav/Kni being on
opposite ends of the spectrum!?) led directly to some thinking and then
the AAPF columns:

AAPF for non-serials:
Val, Wiz, Mon, Sam, Bar, Arc, Tou, Hea, Pri, Ran, Rog, Kni, Cav

and, of course, the 1st Ascension column:
Wiz, Val, Bar, Tou, Sam, Mon, Arc, Pri, Rog, Ran, Hea, Kni, Cav

...leading us to some very definite conclusions:

- -- The average player's first ascension is by far a Valkyrie or
Wizard... and the huge gap between these two and the rest of the field
is a very strong indicator that these two are, in fact, the easiest
classes to ascend for a newbie.

- -- Monk-and-Ninja disease infests NAO to a degree.  (In most other
RPG-like games, the 'monk' and 'ninja' classes are extremely powerful;
new players are drawn to this in NH expecting the same to to be true.
It doesn't look like an accident that Monks are the 3rd most common role
selected.)

- -- Tourists are much easier than everyone gives them credit for.
Apparently the PYEC and quest levels actually are that much of a
benefit, to where a tourist who survives the early game is in great shape.

- -- Healers may be an easier class than is often portrayed.  This makes
some sense since the Healer's quest artifact is so good.

- -- Caveman and Knight are unpopular roles; a player doesn't tend to
ascend them until they have several ascensions under their belt.  I
don't think Caveman and Knights are the two hardest roles, by a long
shot; I think they're just inherently uninteresting and boring.  This is
somewhat supported by the sheer lack of play the roles get compared to
everything else... and frankly, there just isn't enough data on these
two.  When the average player has ascended 3+ times before ever
succeeding with a Caveman or Knight, it's patently obvious that the
people ascending these classes are no longer even remotely newbies...
and thus the high ascension percentage.  By the time you play a Caveman,
you already know how to win.

